@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "ARM Templates vs Terraform";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">When implementing Infrastructure as Code (IaC) in Azure, two popular tools are **Azure Resource Manager (ARM) Templates** and **Terraform**. Understanding their differences helps teams choose the best solution for infrastructure automation.</p>
    <hr />

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What Are ARM Templates?</h2>
        <p>ARM Templates are **declarative JSON files** used to define and deploy Azure infrastructure in a **repeatable and consistent** manner.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Built specifically for **Azure**.</li>
            <li>Provides **idempotent deployments** (ensures the same result every time).</li>
            <li>Supports **parameters, variables, and linked templates**.</li>
            <li>Integration with **Azure Policy** and **RBAC** for compliance.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre>
{
  "$schema": "https://schema.management.azure.com/schemas/2019-04-01/deploymentTemplate.json#",
  "contentVersion": "1.0.0.0",
  "resources": [
    {
      "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts",
      "apiVersion": "2022-09-01",
      "name": "mystorageaccount",
      "location": "eastus",
      "properties": {}
    }
  ]
}
        </pre>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What Is Terraform?</h2>
        <p>Terraform is an **open-source, multi-cloud** IaC tool that allows users to define infrastructure in a **declarative language (HCL - HashiCorp Configuration Language)**.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Supports **multi-cloud deployments** (Azure, AWS, GCP).</li>
            <li>Uses **state files** to track infrastructure changes.</li>
            <li>Provides **modularity and reusability** through Terraform modules.</li>
            <li>Facilitates **drift detection and updates** using `terraform plan` and `terraform apply`.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre>
provider "azurerm" {
  features {}
}

resource "azurerm_storage_account" "mystorage" {
  name                     = "mystorageaccount"
  location                 = "East US"
  resource_group_name      = "myResourceGroup"
  account_tier             = "Standard"
  account_replication_type = "LRS"
}
        </pre>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Key Differences: ARM Templates vs Terraform</h2>
        <table class="table table-bordered text-center">
            <thead class="table-dark">
                <tr>
                    <th>Feature</th>
                    <th>ARM Templates</th>
                    <th>Terraform</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Scope</strong></td>
                    <td>Azure-only</td>
                    <td>Multi-cloud (Azure, AWS, GCP)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Language</strong></td>
                    <td>JSON</td>
                    <td>HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>State Management</strong></td>
                    <td>Azure-managed</td>
                    <td>Uses **state files** for tracking changes</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Modularity</strong></td>
                    <td>Complex and requires linked templates</td>
                    <td>Supports reusable **Terraform modules**</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Execution Plan</strong></td>
                    <td>Deploys without a built-in preview</td>
                    <td>Provides `terraform plan` for previewing changes</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Integration</strong></td>
                    <td>Deeply integrated with Azure security & compliance</td>
                    <td>Requires external authentication mechanisms</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>When to Choose ARM Templates vs Terraform</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Use ARM Templates if:</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>You work **exclusively in Azure**.</li>
                    <li>You require **tight integration with Azure security & policy**.</li>
                    <li>Your team is comfortable with **JSON-based templates**.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Use Terraform if:</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>You need **multi-cloud support**.</li>
                    <li>You want **modular infrastructure code with reusability**.</li>
                    <li>You require **state management & drift detection**.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Both **ARM Templates** and **Terraform** offer powerful ways to define and manage Azure infrastructure as code.
            While ARM Templates are **Azure-native and tightly integrated**, Terraform provides **multi-cloud flexibility and better modularization**.
        </p>
        <p>
            The next step is to explore **Creating Resources with Azure DevOps** for automating IaC deployments.
        </p>
    </div>

